<section class="reference-section" id="bhagiratha-ganga"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Bhagiratha and the Ganga</h2><a href="/bhagiratha-ganga.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Valmiki Ramayana · Bala Kanda 40–44.</strong> Original condensed prose checked against the Sanskrit and Desiraju Hanumanta Rao’s English translation, mirrored at Sanskrit Documents. References follow that presentation’s sarga numbering. Translator introductions, geographical identifications and explanatory notes are commentary, distinct from the verses.</p><ol><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/baala/sarga40/balasans40.htm">Sarga 40 · Sagara’s sons and Kapila</a><small>Verses 25–30: the horse, accusation and destruction.</small></li><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/baala/sarga41/balasans41.htm">Sarga 41 · Anshuman and Garuda</a><small>Verses 12–21: ashes, grief and the need for Ganga’s waters.</small></li><li><a href="https://valmikiramayan.net/utf8/baala/sarga42/balaroman42.htm">Sarga 42 · The generations and Bhagiratha’s request</a><small>Anshuman, Dilipa, the five-fire austerity, Brahma’s boon and the need for Shiva.</small></li><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/baala/sarga43/balasans43.htm">Sarga 43 · Shiva, the descent and Jahnu</a><small>Shiva’s locks, seven streams, the chariot-led journey, Jahnavi and the ancestors.</small></li><li><a href="https://www.valmiki.iitk.ac.in/sloka?field_kanda_tid=1&amp;field_sarga_value=43&amp;language=ro">IIT Kanpur · Bala Kanda 43</a><small>Additional primary-text presentation for the onward journey and Jahnu episode.</small></li><li><a href="https://sanskritdocuments.org/sites/valmikiramayan/baala/sarga44/balasans44.htm">Sarga 44 · Brahma’s praise and ancestral rites</a><small>Fulfilment, the name Bhagirathi, water offerings and return to the capital.</small></li></ol><p>The account is narrated by Vishvamitra to Rama. Vast timescales and the extended family prehistory are condensed. Sacred-water claims are presented as the text’s devotional account. The two new paintings are AI-generated, with invented landscape and chariot details; the reflection is editorial.</p></div></section>
